 "note": "Hook's own landform records, in the scale model's format (tools/scale-model/model/data/landforms.json: px, py on the 5.1 canvas, heights absolute; the Ring Sea at -1500.19 m). The model's 2 km cells hold no land at Hook's pin (sm3ft5vrof, px 809,923): it is a wet cell on a drowned flat, -10 to +5 m for some 8 km, under the plateau 1.5 km east. The records say Hook stands on a peninsula and a spit (populations-gemini.json 'Hook peninsula', GEOLOGY-SANITY-CHECK.md 'Hook spit', a carbonate shelf; every net51 trunk out of Hook starts with a causeway), so the ground is drawn here as core/world 41l outlined benches (the owner's site plan of 2026-11-24, placement B: plan/hook-site-plan.html): the drowned downtown (cut only), the walled peninsula with the north ridge (the spit's id, its quay edge held), the mainland's coastal strips (fill only, tapering to points past the city limits), and two domed hills. px, py, l_m and w_m are the outline's box, for a reader that takes rectangles only. settlements/hook/world/cut.py adds each record to the cut's region.json unless the model already carries its id (cut.py --landforms rewrites only these records); the same records are written up for the scale model's edit queue (the coordinator), after which the model carries them and this file can go. The Heights and the hill behind are the town's, not the map's: they stay the Port kit's stamps in src/76-hook-ground.js.",
